{
  "id": "longest-subarray-of-ones-after-deleting-one",
  "questionId": "longest-subarray-of-ones-after-deleting-one",
  "slug": "longest-subarray-of-ones-after-deleting-one",
  "title": "Longest Subarray of 1's After Deleting One Element",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Sliding window",
  "patterns": [
    "sliding window",
    "variable-size window"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "'delete exactly one element' and 'flip at most one 0 to a 1' sound different, but think about what the final window of 1s actually looks like in both cases.",
    "if you allow at most one 0 inside a sliding window (same idea as Max Consecutive Ones III with k=1), what does the window represent once you subtract 1 for the deleted slot?",
    "grow a window to the right, allowing at most one 0 inside it; if a second 0 appears, shrink from the left until only one 0 remains",
    "the answer is the window length minus 1, since one element in that window (a 0, or if there's no 0, any single 1) represents the element you delete"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting that a deletion is mandatory even when the array is already all 1s, which caps the answer at length - 1 in that case",
    "computing the window length directly as the answer instead of subtracting 1 for the deleted element",
    "allowing more than one 0 in the window, or not shrinking correctly when a second 0 appears"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a binary array (only 0s and 1s). You must delete exactly\none element from it.\n\n**Return the length of the longest stretch of consecutive 1s you can\nget in what remains.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^5",
      "nums[i] is either 0 or 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the array is already all 1s (you still must delete one)",
      "the array is all 0s (answer is 0)",
      "a single element array"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
